  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A pleasant Sunday afternoon across the region and \nnortherly winds continue to push lower dew point air southward and \ntemperatures rise towards the 80 degree mark.  The only convective \nfocus into the evening hours will be near the Black Hills region. \n\nTONIGHT: High pressure sinks southward this evening and\novernight, allowing a quick drop in temperatures. As we reach \nthe midnight hour, gradually increasing low-lvl warm advection \nover central SD and northern NE should induce a mid-lvl cloud \ndeck. There may be sufficient lift to produce isolated to \nscattered high based showers and thunderstorms along the MO \nriver, with a secondary wave dropping out of ND also producing a\npocket of elevated convection. Latest trends keep PoPs focused \nalong or west of the James River valley through 12Z, with no \nsevere weather expected. \n\nMONDAY/MONDAY NIGHT:  Strong to severe convection risks remain the \nbiggest story for Monday afternoon and evening. We may start the day \nwith quite a bit of mid-lvl clouds and a few showers near the James \nRiver valley. Should a small area of vorticity continue crossing \nnorthern SD, then associated warm advection may push eastward \ntowards I-29 by mid-day bringing a low risk for sprinkles to high \nbased showers. \n\nBy the afternoon hours we should begin to gradually become more \nunstable as winds turn southwesterly. The degree of instability \nremains in question partially due to the fact that 70 dewpoints \nhave been pushed well south of the CWA, and southerly boundary \nlayer flow only commences by late morning. Secondly, lingering \nmid-lvl clouds could also lower temperature potential. We'll \nbegin watching a front slowly pushing into south central and \nnortheastern SODAK and western MN by late afternoon which may, \nalong with any weak pre- frontal trough, provide enough \nconvergence to develop isolated to scattered convection after \n4pm. Greater convection risks may develop later in the evening \nor early overnight as guidance continues to show weak vorticity \nin the northwesterly flow entering the region inducing a \nstronger LLJ response. It's also possible that the \naforementioned front may further slow down. It should be said \nthough, that this northwesterly flow continues to remain \nproblematic for models to resolve. \n\nFrom a parameter perspective, MLCAPE upwards of 1800-2800 J/KG \n(dependent on T/Td trends and mixing in the afternoon), combined \nwith effective shear AOA 35 knots should produce at least potential \nfor multi-cell/supercellular development capable of golf ball hail \nand 60 mph winds in the evening. Some storm clustering\ntowards straight line wind risks could also develop given the\nunidirectional mid-lvl flow. Tornadic risks will remain low, \nbut not- zero, especially if storm development holds off until \nafter 00z when better boundary layer moisture arrives into the \nTri- State area and very low- lvl flow begins to increase with \nthe developing LLJ. This could push 0-1 SRH upwards of 100 M2/S2\nby 00-03Z. Overall storm coverage remain uncertain given \nsomewhat marginal forcing initially and perhaps some potential \nfor weak inhibition holding on. \n\nWill continue with elevated PoPs through the overnight hours given \nthe potential for increased forcing as noted earlier.  The severe \nweather risks should lessen after 10pm, as mid-lvl lapse rates \nweaken. Though a few gusty storms may continue into Tuesday morning. \n\nTUESDAY: There remains quite a bit of uncertainty regarding \nTuesday's forecast. Should convection persist overnight, this may \ndrive any effect boundary south of the CWA.  However some guidance \ncontinues to hint that any strong convection may develop \nquickly in the morning near the MO river and Highway 20 and \ndrive southward effectively limiting the afternoon potential in \nthe CWA. Temperatures Tuesday cool back into the 70s. \n\nWEDNESDAY-SATURDAY: A larger trough dropping into Hudson Bay by mid-\nweek will push a passing trough eastward through the region on \nWednesday.  Cooler temperatures and lower instability should keep \nthe risk of severe storms low.  Into the weekend, we'll continue to \nlook for near to slightly below normal temperatures, with no focused \narea of high PoPs given a uncertainty of waves in the \nreturning northwesterly flow aloft.",
